# =============================================================================
# web/.env.dev.local.example — what YOU set on your own machine
# =============================================================================
# This file is committed and is NEVER loaded by anything. It is documentation.
#
#   cp web/.env.dev.local.example web/.env.dev.local
#
# `web/.env.dev.local` is gitignored, so it is the only place your personal
# tokens belong. Never put a real value in the file you are reading now.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which file does a value belong in?
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   web/.env                     committed. Safe defaults for every environment.
#                                No real secrets, ever.
#   web/.env.staging|.prod       committed. Non-secret per-environment values.
#                                Loaded ON THE SERVER, never on your laptop.
#                                The `replace-me` entries in there are tripwires:
#                                the deploy refuses to install a file that still
#                                contains one. Do not "fix" them with realistic
#                                values.
#   web/.env.dev.local           YOURS, gitignored. This file's copy. ← you
#   developers/docker/.env       YOURS, gitignored. The dev STACK's config
#                                (ports, Postgres, MinIO, media, coverage).
#                                Start from developers/docker/.env.example.
#   shared/.env.<env>.local      on the servers. Rendered by Ansible from the
#                                vault. Nobody edits it by hand.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE THAT CATCHES EVERYONE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker compose passes its `environment:` entries as REAL process environment
# variables, and a real environment variable beats EVERY Symfony .env* file —
# including this one. So any key listed in the "compose owns these" section at
# the bottom will be silently ignored if you set it here. It will not warn you.
# It will just keep using the compose value.
#
# Rule of thumb: if `developers/docker/compose.yaml` names the key under
# `environment:`, set it in `developers/docker/.env`. Otherwise set it here.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. Personal API tokens — this file is the ONLY place they work
# =============================================================================
# None of these are required to boot. Each one is a feature that stays switched
# off while it is empty, and says so rather than half-working.

# Mapillary access token — street-level imagery lookups.
# Empty = the Mapillary panel is not offered.
# Get one at https://www.mapillary.com/dashboard/developers
MAPILLARY_TOKEN=

# ArcGIS Location Platform key for the satellite base layer.
# Public by design (it ships to the browser) and referrer-restricted at Esri,
# but it is still a billable account key — so it never goes in a committed file.
# Empty = no satellite layer, the rest of the map is unaffected.
ESRI_API_KEY=

# Google Safe Browsing (free Lookup API) for rider-submitted outbound links.
# Empty = the check is OFF and every call site says so, rather than quietly
# passing everything through. Needs no billing account for the free tier.
SAFE_BROWSING_KEY=


# =============================================================================
# 2. Optional — only if your setup differs from the standard dev stack
# =============================================================================
# Delete anything here you are not actually changing. An empty value is a
# DECISION ("feature off"), not the same as leaving the line out (which keeps
# web/.env's default).

# --- Valhalla elevation -------------------------------------------------------
# web/.env already points at the standard SSH-tunnelled instances via
# host.docker.internal. Override only if you tunnel different ports.
# WARNING: the published ports are NOT in alphabetical order — read the comment
# above ELEVATION_URLS in web/.env before you guess one. Probing two continents
# on each other's ports makes a full instance look empty.
#ELEVATION_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8002
#ELEVATION_URLS="north-america=http://host.docker.internal:8003,asia=http://host.docker.internal:8004,oceania=http://host.docker.internal:8007,africa=http://host.docker.internal:8006,south-america=http://host.docker.internal:8005"

# --- Map artifacts ------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty = the matching toggle does not appear in the UI, which is correct until
# a region has actually been built. Set the MANIFEST and the pinned URLs can
# stay empty; a rebuild then goes live with no config change.
#ROAD_SURFACE_MANIFEST_URL=
#ROAD_SURFACE_TILES_URL=
#ROAD_SURFACE_TODO_URL=
#SURFACE_GAPS_URL=
#ROUTES_MANIFEST_URL=
#ROUTES_TILES_URL=

# --- Cosmetic -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Footer build stamp. Leave unset: when git is reachable the stamp comes from
# `git describe --tags`, which is what you want locally.
#APP_BUILD_VERSION=
# Moderator rulebook PDF. Owner-managed on the server; empty locally just means
# the rulebook page shows no download link.
#CC_RULEBOOK_PDF_URL=


# =============================================================================
# 3. DO NOT SET THESE HERE — docker compose owns them
# =============================================================================
# Listed so you can stop hunting. compose.yaml injects each of these as a real
# environment variable, which overrides this file. Setting them here does
# nothing at all, silently.
#
# This is the list for the `app` and `worker` containers — i.e. the keys Symfony
# actually reads, which are the only ones you might think to put in this file.
# The other services (db, minio, mailpit, pipeline) have their own keys —
# POSTGRES_*, MINIO_*, COVERAGE_S3_*, DEM_DIR and friends — and those are
# documented in developers/docker/.env.example. Symfony never reads them.
#
# Change them in `developers/docker/.env` instead:
#
#   APP_ENV  APP_DEBUG  APP_SECRET  APP_CACHE_DIR  APP_LOG_DIR
#   DATABASE_URL  REDIS_URL  MAILER_DSN  MESSENGER_TRANSPORT_DSN
#   COVERAGE_TILES  COVERAGE_MANIFEST_URL  COVERAGE_CSP_HOST
#   MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT  MEDIA_S3_REGION  MEDIA_S3_KEY  MEDIA_S3_SECRET
#   MEDIA_S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET  MEDIA_S3_BUCKET_PRIVATE
#   MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE  MEDIA_CSP_HOST   
#   CLAMAV_TCP_ADDR  CLAMAV_REQUIRED   (worker container only)
#
# Two of those deserve a warning:
#
# APP_SECRET — besides signing, it derives the AES-256-GCM key for exactly ONE
# column: users.totp_secret, the 2FA authenticator seed
# (src/Doctrine/EncryptedStringType.php, registered as `encrypted_string` in
# config/packages/doctrine.yaml). Nothing else in the database is encrypted —
# that is by design, and it is why a query browser shows you every other field
# in the clear. Change APP_SECRET and any user with 2FA enabled can no longer
# log in with their authenticator; they have to re-enrol. Keep the shared dev
# default unless you accept that.
#
# MEDIA_S3_* — the dev defaults derive from MINIO_ROOT_USER / MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD
# so there is exactly one place to change them. To point at a MinIO you already
# run on the host, override them in developers/docker/.env, not here. Note the
# two hostnames are not interchangeable: the app writes server-side over the
# compose network (minio:9000) while the browser reads the published port
# (localhost:9100).
